To estimate the consumption of disposable wooden chopsticks, in 1999, a sample of 10 restaurants from a total of 600 high, medium, and low-grade restaurants in a certain county was taken. The daily consumption of disposable chopstick boxes in these restaurants was as follows:  
0.6, 3.7, 2.2, 1.5, 2.8, 1.7, 1.2, 2.1, 3.2, 1.0  
(1) Estimate the total consumption of disposable chopstick boxes in the county for the year 1999 by calculating the sample (assuming 350 business days per year);  
(2) In 2001, another survey on the consumption of disposable wooden chopsticks was conducted in the same manner, and the result was that the average daily use of disposable chopstick boxes in the 10 sampled restaurants was 2.42 boxes. Calculate the average annual growth rate of the consumption of disposable wooden chopsticks for the years 2000 and 2001 (the number of restaurants in the county and the total number of business days in the year remained the same as in 1999);  
(3) Under the conditions of (2), if producing a set of desks and chairs for primary and secondary school students requires 0.07 cubic meters of wood, calculate how many sets of student desks and chairs can be produced with the wood used for disposable chopsticks in the county in 2001. The relevant data needed for the calculation are: 100 pairs of chopsticks per box, each pair of chopsticks weighs 5 grams, and the density of the wood used is 0.5×10<sup>3</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup>;  
(4) If you were asked to estimate the amount of wood consumed by disposable chopsticks in your province for a year, how would you use statistical knowledge to do so? Briefly describe in words.

gpt_answer:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(1)}\quad &\bar{y}_{1999}=\frac{0.6+3.7+2.2+1.5+2.8+1.7+1.2+2.1+3.2+1.0}{10}=\frac{20}{10}=2.0\\
&\hat{T}_{1999}=600\times 350\times 2.0=4.2\times 10^5\ \text{boxes}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(2)}\quad &\bar{y}_{2001}=2.42,\quad \bar{y}_{1999}=2.00\\
&\text{Average annual growth rate } r=\left(\frac{\bar{y}_{2001}}{\bar{y}_{1999}}\right)^{1/2}-1=\left(\frac{2.42}{2.00}\right)^{1/2}-1=\sqrt{1.21}-1=0.10=10\%
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(3)}\quad &\hat{T}_{2001}=600\times 350\times 2.42=508{,}200\ \text{boxes}\\
&\text{Pairs}=508{,}200\times 100=50{,}820{,}000\ \text{pairs}\\
&\text{Mass}=50{,}820{,}000\times 5\ \text{g}=254{,}100{,}000\ \text{g}=254{,}100\ \text{kg}\\
&\text{Volume}=\frac{254{,}100}{0.5\times 10^{3}}=\frac{254{,}100}{500}=508.2\ \text{m}^3\\
&\text{Sets of desks/chairs}=\frac{508.2}{0.07}=7{,}260\ \text{sets}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(4)}\quad &\textbf{Approach A (stratified restaurant survey):}\\
&\text{1) Define strata (e.g., by city/county and restaurant grade); let }N_h\text{ be restaurants in stratum }h.\\
&\text{2) Sample }n_h\text{ restaurants per stratum; within each, sample }d\text{ days to get }y_{hid}.\\
&\text{3) Compute }\bar{y}_{hi}=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{t=1}^{d}y_{hid},\ \bar{y}_h=\frac{1}{n_h}\sum_{i=1}^{n_h}\bar{y}_{hi}.\\
&\text{4) Estimate total boxes: }\hat{T}_{\text{box}}=D\sum_h N_h\,\bar{y}_h\ \ (\text{with }D\text{ business days/year}).\\
&\text{5) Convert to wood volume: }\hat{V}=\frac{\hat{T}_{\text{box}}\times m\times \hat{w}}{\rho},\ \ (m=\text{pairs/box},\ \hat{w}=\text{avg. mass/pair},\ \rho=\text{density}).\\
&\text{6) Optional output: sets }=\hat{V}/0.07.\\[4pt]
&\textbf{Approach B (supplier-based):}\\
&\text{1) Sample wholesalers/manufacturers; collect annual box sales }Y_j.\\
&\text{2) Use Horvitz–Thompson or ratio/regression estimator (with known frame totals) for }\hat{T}_{\text{box}}.\\
&\text{3) Convert to }\hat{V}\text{ and sets as above.}
\end{aligned}
\]

